 \markboth{Smart Safety Tag}{Smart Safety Tag}
 \begin{abstract}
-Smart Safety Tag is a prototype for sharing an emergency alert with trusted contacts. The project combines an Android-first application, an application programming interface, an administrative portal and designs for a custom wearable PCB and charging adapter. The app supports a confirmed SOS action and guardian management; the backend stores emergency events and provides best-effort notifications. The tag design includes an ESP32-S3 module, a button, motion and audio peripherals, local feedback and a protected charging path. This article describes the design and distinguishes software checks and engineering calculations from physical measurements. The available checks include 24 passing mobile unit tests, 57 passing backend tests using in-memory repositories and a successful administrative type check. Saved design reports record no PCB design-rule violations, and the hardware-test firmware has been compiled. No assembled tag was available for physical validation, and the wearable emergency connection and keyword inference remain unimplemented. The work therefore documents a design and its current evidence; it does not establish improved emergency response or the performance of a complete wearable system.
+Smart Safety Tag is a personal-safety prototype intended to alert trusted contacts during an emergency. Its components are an Android-first application, an application programming interface, an administrative portal, and designs for a custom wearable PCB and its charging adapter. A user can confirm an SOS in the app and manage guardians; the backend records emergencies and attempts notifications on a best-effort basis. The proposed tag combines an ESP32-S3 module and button with motion and audio peripherals, local feedback and a protected charging path. We describe the design alongside the evidence available for each part. That evidence includes 24 passing mobile unit tests, 57 passing backend tests with in-memory repositories, and a successful administrative type check. Saved PCB reports show no design-rule violations, and the hardware-test firmware has compiled. An assembled tag was not available for physical validation, however, and neither the wearable emergency connection nor keyword inference has been implemented. These results characterize the current prototype and design; they do not demonstrate improved emergency response or the performance of an integrated wearable system.
 \end{abstract}
 \begin{keywords}Personal safety, wearable devices, emergency notification, Bluetooth Low Energy, mobile applications, prototype verification.\end{keywords}
